---
name: nadia-tools-check
version: 0.1.0
description: Drives the tool handlers against the inputs a model actually sends wrong; a tool must answer, never raise.
---

# Contract check for the tool surface

```bash
ssc run src/tools-check.ssc          # exit 0 = every wrong input got an ANSWER
```

`SPEC.md` §2: *a tool error is not an exception — it is the next prompt.* A handler that
raises does not fail the call, it kills the run, and the model never gets to correct itself.

This check exists because that promise was broken here and nowhere else. `read_file` and
`edit_file` guarded with `exists`, which is **true for a directory** — so a model asking to
read a directory died with `Is a directory` while the Rust twin returned
`read X: Is a directory (os error 21)` and the Scala 3 twin `read X: …`. Both of those are
sentences a model can act on. Found 2026-08-04 while making the fs reads total.

The cases are the ones a model gets wrong on its own: a directory where a file was meant, a
path that is not there, and a path outside the jail.

[Sandbox, nadiaTools](tools.ssc)
[mkdirs, writeFile, deleteFile](std/fs.ssc)
[exit](std/os.ssc)

```scalascript
var failures = 0

def check(name: String, ok: Boolean): Unit =
  if ok then println("  ok   " + name)
  else
    println("  FAIL " + name)
    failures = failures + 1

val root = "/tmp/nadia-tools-check"
mkdirs(root + "/adir")
writeFile(root + "/a.txt", "hello")

val sb = Sandbox(root)
val tools = nadiaTools(sb)

def call(name: String, argsJson: String): (Boolean, String) =
  tools.find(t => t.name == name) match
    case Some(t) =>
      val r = t.handler(argsJson)
      (r.isError, r.contentJson)
    case None => (true, "no such tool " + name)

println("nadia tools — every wrong input must ANSWER, not raise")

// The defect this file was written for: a directory where a file was meant.
val (dirErr, dirMsg) = call("read_file", "{\"path\":\"adir\"}")
check("read_file on a directory answers with an error", dirErr)
check("...and the error says WHICH mistake it was", dirMsg.contains("directory"))

val (editErr, editMsg) =
  call("edit_file", "{\"path\":\"adir\",\"old_string\":\"a\",\"new_string\":\"b\"}")
check("edit_file on a directory answers with an error", editErr)
check("...and names the mistake too", editMsg.contains("directory"))

// A path that is not there — the case that was already handled, kept so a fix cannot
// regress it into the directory branch.
val (missErr, missMsg) = call("read_file", "{\"path\":\"nope.txt\"}")
check("read_file on a missing path answers with an error", missErr)
check("...and says no such file, not 'is a directory'",
      missMsg.contains("no such file") && !missMsg.contains("directory"))

// Outside the jail: refused, and the refusal says so rather than clamping.
val (outErr, outMsg) = call("read_file", "{\"path\":\"../../etc/passwd\"}")
check("a path outside the workspace is refused", outErr)
check("...and the refusal says outside, not 'no such file'", outMsg.contains("outside"))

// list_dir on a FILE, and on nothing at all.
val (ldErr, _) = call("list_dir", "{\"path\":\"a.txt\"}")
check("list_dir on a file answers with an error", ldErr)
val (ldMissErr, _) = call("list_dir", "{\"path\":\"nope\"}")
check("list_dir on a missing path answers with an error", ldMissErr)

// And the happy paths still work — a tool that errors on everything would pass all of the above.
val (okErr, okMsg) = call("read_file", "{\"path\":\"a.txt\"}")
check("read_file on a real file succeeds", !okErr && okMsg.contains("hello"))
val (lsErr, lsMsg) = call("list_dir", "{\"path\":\".\"}")
check("list_dir on the root succeeds", !lsErr && lsMsg.contains("a.txt"))

deleteFile(root + "/a.txt")

println("")
if failures == 0 then
  println("the tool surface answers")
  exit(0)
else
  println(failures.toString + " case(s) raise or mislead — SPEC §2 says a tool error is the next prompt")
  exit(1)
```
